WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.600
The third episode of Game of Thrones’ eighth
season saw Arya Stark go from a woman to a

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.100
warrior.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.720
Entitled "The Long Night," the latest chapter
in Game of Thrones' final season centered

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.250
on the battle between the Night King and his
Army of Dead and the living citizens of Westeros

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.840
holding strong at Winterfell.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.020
The episode also featured more incredible
moments than we can count on one hand.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.940
Lots of spoilers beyond this point, obviously.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.940
The Red Priestess Melisandre made a surprise
return, uniting fire with ice by calling on

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.640
the Lord of Light to set the Dothraki outriders'
weapons on fire.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.140
Daenerys Targaryen and Jon Snow rode atop
their respective dragons and attempted to

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.440
burn out the enemy with flames from above.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.840
Several characters, like little Lyanna Mormont
and Jorah Mormont sacrificed themselves battling

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:45.400
the wights.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.679
Theon Greyjoy died defending Bran Stark, and
the dead in the crypts of Winterfell rose

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.840
and began a terrifying attack on innocent
women, children, and elderly citizens.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.020
As intense as these events surrounding the
Battle of Winterfell were, they felt like

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.800
small fish compared to what Arya Stark did
at the end of the episode.

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:07.539
The youngest Stark sister made "The Long Night"
that much more memorable by ending the very

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.780
event for which the entire episode was named.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.230
In a moment few saw coming, Arya jumped at
the Night King and stabbed him, driving her

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.020
Valyrian steel dagger through an exposed section
of his armor.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.079
The second the blade made contact with the
frosty villain's abdomen, the Night King shattered

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.680
to smithereens - as did his army of White
Walkers and wights.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.799
Arya’s heroic surprise stunt dropped viewers'
jaws and elicited screams of delight from

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.240
Game of Thrones fans.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.300
As the episode aired live on HBO, viewers
lit up social media with real-time reactions

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.800
to Arya slaying the Night King.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.140
One fan tweeted:

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:47.200
"My head hurts from screaming since the episode
started but man… this moment was just amazing!

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.320
Arya Stark, well done queen.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.590
Arya killing the night king is probably one
of the most iconic moments in this show.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.020
A truly amazing scene."

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:00.480
Game of Thrones fan Cameron Stokes could barely
contain his emotions over Arya stabbing the

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.860
Night King and defeating the North’s greatest
threat.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.480
Stokes posted to Twitter:

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.680
"Tonight was simply the most incredible 125
min in tv history, Arya is my favorite TV

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:11.960
character of all time…

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:12.800
That is all."

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.171
Evidence suggests that Arya's true destiny
was to slaughter the Night King and his army

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.800
- and this seemed to be confirmed during a
chat she had with Melisandre.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.200
Before setting out to save the day - or night,
as it happens - Arya finds herself face-to-face

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:30.120
with the Red Woman, who brings up the notion
of purpose in regard to Beric Dondarrion,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.360
the man who sacrificed himself protecting
Arya from wights in the library of Winterfell.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.440
Melisandre reminds Arya of Beric’s history
of dying multiple times, only to be brought

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.280
back to life by the Lord of Light - suggesting
that he had been kept alive so he could save

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.160
Arya during the Battle of Winterfell, ensuring
she would be alive to defeat the Night King.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.020
During their conversation, Arya corroborates
Melisandre's claim of soothsaying when she

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.070
remembers that the Red Woman previously prophesied
that the youngest Stark girl would take the

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.560
lives of many people.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:06.220
This eerie prophecy happened all the way back
in season 3, when Arya confronted Melisandre

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.080
for taking her best friend, Gendry, away for
blood magic purposes.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.880
Melisandre told Arya she saw a darkness in
the young girl, before alluding to the number

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.740
of people she would go on to kill:

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.520
"Brown eyes, blue eyes, green eyes.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.640
Eyes you’ll shut forever."

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:25.860
"We will meet again."

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.720
Melisandre repeats her prophecy during the
third episode of season 8, rewording the phrase

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.760
about the eyes to place emphasis on the blue
ones that Arya will shut.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.280
The scrappy Stark girl seems to take this
to mean that Melisandre has seen into a future

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.420
in which she kills the Night King, who has
blue eyes.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.560
Arya's mind is made up already, but Melisandre
seals the deal when she asks Arya the same

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.020
question her first sword-fighting instructor
Syrio Forel used to:

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.800
"What do we say to 
the God of Death?"

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.280
"Not today."

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.820
Arya’s killing of the Night King could impact
another of major prophecy that Melisandre

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:11.540
often spoke of: Azor Ahai, also known as the
Prince That Was Promised, who is prophesied

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.100
to end the Long Night and provide Westeros
with everlasting peace.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.100
Melisandre told Daenerys during season 7 that
"only the Prince Who Was Promised can bring

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.980
the dawn," and that she believed Dany had
"a role to play" in the Long Night ending.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.190
She also shared with the Mother of Dragons
that Jon Snow would be pivotal to ringing

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.180
in a new era - but there was no mention of
Arya.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.300
So, does this mean the Azor Ahai prophecy
is… pointless?

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.860
Maybe, but then again, maybe not.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:42.520
Arya still has a set of green eyes to shut
- green eyes many fans believe belong to Cersei

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.420
Lannister who’s presumably sitting cool
as a cucumber down in King's Landing.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.560
Some are theorizing that maybe the Long Night
isn't over just yet and that it will only

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:57.280
come to an end when Arya kills the real threat
to Westeros: Cersei and her big green eyes.

